INT. GRAND HOTEL - SERVICE CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Cream wallpaper, a trolley of silver domes, a flickering exit sign. The corridor smells of lilies and floor wax.

A woman in a maid's uniform, thick glasses, pushes a linen cart. This is ROSALIE VANCE (40s, the inside woman). She stops at a door marked STAFF ONLY, glances both ways, and slips a keycard from her sleeve.

She taps in. Opens. Behind her, a man in a bellboy cap steps out of an alcove. This is DEVLIN OKONJO (30s, wiry, quick hands). He tips the cap, nods once, and slides in beside her.

Inside, a wall of monitors. A security desk, empty. A paper cup of tea steams on the counter.

She pulls a USB stick from her bra. He plugs it into the terminal. The screens stutter, loop, and settle into a calm still of the lobby.

ROSALIE
Eleven minutes.

DEVLIN
I need ten.

ROSALIE
You said eight.

DEVLIN
He said eight. I said ten.

A phone on the desk rings. Both freeze. They stare at it. It rings again. She reaches out, hesitates, picks up.

ROSALIE
(into phone, bright)
Security.

MANAGER (V.O.)
Rosalie? Why are you in the monitor room?

ROSALIE
(a beat)
Cleaning, sir.

MANAGER (V.O.)
At this hour?

ROSALIE
Sir, the carpet. There was a spill. I am almost finished.

MANAGER (V.O.)
Where's Hector?

ROSALIE
Hector went to the restroom, sir.

MANAGER (V.O.)
Tell him to call me.

ROSALIE
Of course.

She hangs up. Devlin mimes wiping his brow. She smacks his arm. He stifles a laugh.

On the screen, the vault door in the basement glows with a green LED. Devlin leans in and types.

DEVLIN
That's the lock.

ROSALIE
Don't touch it yet.

DEVLIN
Why not?

ROSALIE
Because someone's coming.

Footsteps in the corridor, brisk and heavy. They scramble. She shoves the cart against the door. He ducks under the desk. She grabs a rag and kneels, scrubbing the carpet, as the handle turns and a heavy shape fills the doorway: HECTOR PRIETO (50s, security guard, toothpick in his mouth).

HECTOR
Rosalie? What are you doing in here?

ROSALIE
Cleaning, Hector.

HECTOR
At this hour?

ROSALIE
There was a spill.

He looks at the monitors. The lobby, frozen. He looks at her. At the cart. At the desk.

HECTOR
Where's the tea?

ROSALIE
What tea?

He points at the counter. The cup is gone. Under the desk, a gloved hand slowly lowers it to the floor.

Hector turns slowly. Rosalie stands. They stare at each other, two people who know exactly what they know, and neither says it.
